# Duetto No. 3 in G major, BWV 804 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: the third of the four Duetti from Clavier-Uebung III
# (1739), and the gentlest: a two-part invention in a lilting 12/8,
# almost a pastorale or gigue at walking pace. The subject is a little
# falling sixteenth run that ends in a leap (G, F-sharp, E, D, then B, A),
# tossed between the hands; the counter-line flows in sixteenths.
#
# Time signature: 12/8, four dotted-quarter beats to the bar; 39 bars,
# the last a whole note (Mutopia Bach-Duet3.mid / Bach-Duet3.ly). No
# upbeat (the top's first beat is a rest under the bass's broken
# chord), no repeats. The MIDI holds 38 2/3 bars.
#
# Tempo: dotted quarter = 54, about 2:52. Track times (listing order
# taken as BWV 802-805): Ralph Kirkpatrick (harpsichord, 1952) 3:02,
# about dotted quarter = 51, and (Archiv, 1959) 2:53, about 54; Ton
# Koopman (organ, Teldec, 1996) 2:41, about 58; Helmut Walcha
# (harpsichord, 1951) 3:20, about 46; Rosalyn Tureck (piano, 1959)
# 3:45, about 41. Source: the bach-cantatas.com discographies. 54
# follows Kirkpatrick's 1959 recording: it lilts, and the sixteenths
# (11 frames each) run clearly.
#
# Structure (my reading of the score and the MIDI):
#   1-2    subject in the top over the bass's broken G major chord.
#   3-4    subject in the bass, the top running above.
#   5-6    sequences.
#   7-10   subject in the bass (7) and top (9); cadence in D major at
#          10-11.
#   11-15  long tied notes in the top over the bass's figures,
#          falling by step: suspensions, toward E minor.
#   16-19  subject in the bass (16) and top (18), E minor.
#   20-23  held notes again, B minor, then toward C.
#   24-27  subject in the bass in C major (24), sequences.
#   28-33  subject in the bass back in G (28); the top's long run.
#   34-39  last entries (top 37) over dominant-seventh harmony; final
#          cadence in G.
#
# Plan: terraced by section at the cadences; the subject brought
# forward where it enters in each hand, which is most of the shaping a
# two-part piece needs. Bass notes below B2 are lifted an octave by the
# converter.

tempo 54 4.

voice mid 0           # two voices only

dyn 1 mf              # bars 1-10: exposition in G
lead 1-2 top          # subject, top
lead 3-4 bottom       # subject, bass
lead 7-8 bottom       # subject, bass
lead 9-10 top         # subject, top
broaden 10 0.95       # bar 10: into the D major cadence

dyn 11 mp             # bars 11-23: the suspensions, E minor, B minor
lead 16-17 bottom     # subject, bass, E minor
lead 18-19 top        # subject, top, E minor

dyn 24 mf             # bars 24-27: subject in C major
lead 24-25 bottom

dyn 28 f              # bars 28-39: the subject home in G, to the end
lead 28-29 bottom
lead 37-38 top        # last entry, top

rit 1.5 0.7           # from the middle of bar 38 into the final G
